Putting this in writing since it keeps coming up in review and I keep talking=
 around it without actually landing the point.

A cache write is an optimisation for the next run. If it fails, the next run =
costs more. It does not make the current run invalid.

The line I want us to hold: nothing that has already cost the user money gets=
 aborted because we couldn't put a file on disk. The operations that go out t=
o the provider are billed. Once that call has been made and the response is b=
ack, real money has been spent. Aborting the run at that point because we the=
n couldn't persist the result is not an acceptable tradeoff. We take the hit =
on the rerun and carry on.

I wrote up the broader keying position =E2=80=94 selective re-issue by row, n=
ot full invalidation =E2=80=94 on the wiki under design, "Prompt-level respon=
se cache keying". This is the failure-handling side of the same topic.

Happy to walk through either part if useful.
